{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>The daughter of a Canaanite man.</b> Meaning, a merchant. And the Gaon Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra wrote: ״And it is possible that it is as it sounds.״ But I say that it is not possible that the sons of Jacob would attach themselves to something that their three forefathers together insisted upon. As they say in Pesaḥim (Pesaḥim 50a), and Scripture guarded the matter by being precise to say ״the daughter of a Canaanite man״ — meaning, her father was a Canaanite but she herself was not a Canaanite woman. And in what sense would the matter hold if you do not say [it means] a merchant? For if he were an actual Canaanite, and Scripture were informing us that Judah did something improper, it should have said: ״וַיַּרְא שָׁם בַּת כְּנַעֲנִית״ or ״אִשָּׁה כְּנַעֲנִית וְשֵׁם אָבִיהָ שׁוּעַ״. And there is no extra word beyond what the text wrote; and for a matter of such disclosure it is fitting to be silent — and to write, indeed." "<b>And he took her.</b> Meaning, a lawful taking of a wife."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "The phrase וּבְאֵיזֶה אוֹפֶן יִהְיֶה הַדָּבָר is translated hyper-literally; the logical thrust is: 'in what way would the matter make sense [if he were a Canaanite],' unless 'merchant' is understood."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:54.677885322Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}